                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "f05ed9b4-0749-340e-b764-d3c0050fc95d",
                        "entry_id": "8ec3403d-4c73-3bf4-a59e-73328536cc3e",
                        "glosses": [
                            "die Kraft, die in einem Organismus liegt und seine Entwicklung oder Selbstverwirklichung bewirkt oder lenkt"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "16765880-bd77-4829-b19f-414b024f77ea",
                                "sense_id": "f05ed9b4-0749-340e-b764-d3c0050fc95d",
                                "text": "„Die Seele bestimmte Aristoteles als erste Entelechie eines organischen Körpers.“"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "041c9551-64bb-4ee9-83eb-35c299d6094b",
                                "sense_id": "f05ed9b4-0749-340e-b764-d3c0050fc95d",
                                "text": "„Die Gesellschaft, deren Teil er ist, bedingt nicht ihn, sondern sie ist nur, insofern sie einen Bezug hat zu seiner Entelechie.“"
                            }
                        ],
                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "bfd5e9f9-3bc5-4aea-a8ae-877dc3a0ee5e",
                        "source_entry_id": "8ec3403d-4c73-3bf4-a59e-73328536cc3e",
                        "source_sense_id": "f05ed9b4-0749-340e-b764-d3c0050fc95d",
                        "source_sense_description": "Kraft, die in einem Organismus liegt und seine Entwicklung oder Selbstverwirklichung bewirkt oder lenkt",
                        "target_lang_word": "entelechy",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "entelechy",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "608268bd-7098-3b73-83ed-f110a10854c4",
                                        "entry_id": "a79ebe71-42e2-3471-bc74-73737c1711c8",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The complete realisation and final form of some potential concept or function; the conditions under which a potential thing becomes actualized."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c9813f9b-61fe-4b66-8f0d-a0495442cbb1",
                                                "sense_id": "608268bd-7098-3b73-83ed-f110a10854c4",
                                                "text": "[Pages 114–115] [H]e [Aristotle] tells us expressly, that that which we call the rational soul is […] 'separable from the body,' […] 'because it is not the entelech of any body.' […] [page 500] Wickedness is the form and entelech of all the wicked spirits: it is the difference of a name, rather than any proper difference of natures that is between the devil and wicked men."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "cbbf2fbb-b3bb-4a31-95b4-509c5ceaa06d",
                                                "sense_id": "608268bd-7098-3b73-83ed-f110a10854c4",
                                                "text": "Act, as we have already emphasized, is also called by Aristotle an entelechy. […] [A]ct and entelechy bespeak something fulfilled, actualized perfection, or the actualized. The soul, hence, insofar as it is essence or form of the body, is the act and entelechy of the body; and in general all forms of sensible substances are act and entelechy. God, we will see, will be pure entelechy (and just as the other movent Intelligences of the celestial spheres)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "40ec79bb-8223-49c2-8c37-03c5c4b93518",
                                                "sense_id": "608268bd-7098-3b73-83ed-f110a10854c4",
                                                "text": "Simply put, entelechy is any process that has a beginning, middle, and end. The end or goal is the telos; it shows up as the second syllable of the word en-TEL-echy. A simple natural entelechy is the corn entelechy. A kernel of corn when planted begins an entelechy. That is, a process of growth begins as the kernel/seed sprouts roots, then a blade. The growth process continues until it reaches its telos. Its telos is the completion of the process and the resulting production of brand new kernels that look very much like the kernel that began this process. Once that telos or goal has been reached, we say that the process is complete or perfect or teleios. But then what happens? A new entelechy begins. Each of those kernels that were produced in the previous entelechy begins to sprout and produces its own entelechy. In other words, entelechies are often cyclical."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f8cc27e2-ffd6-4fdc-a8c8-1dcc939198a6",
                                                "sense_id": "608268bd-7098-3b73-83ed-f110a10854c4",
                                                "text": "But while these forces of chance work both in organic and in inorganic nature, there is an additional principle in organic (living) nature: an organizing principle or entelechie."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9a8777cb-3ede-4562-8799-d209c27c3026",
                                                "sense_id": "608268bd-7098-3b73-83ed-f110a10854c4",
                                                "text": "Aristotleians assumed that rest is the natural state of a body in the terrestrial sphere. If something moves, then this unnatural change in its state requires explanation. Something must be pushing on it, or its motions must derive from some internal teleological \"drive\" within it – a sort of simple consciousness motivating it. These internal drives were called \"entelechies,\" and Aristotleians held that the entire universe is imbued with entelechies."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "f8af05f0-b5e0-325e-a8e8-fd90cb9a54d9",
                                        "entry_id": "a79ebe71-42e2-3471-bc74-73737c1711c8",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The complete realisation and final form of some potential concept or function; the conditions under which a potential thing becomes actualized.",
                                            "In the metaphysics of Aristotle (384–322 B.C.E.) and Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz (1646–1716): a soul; a monad (Leibniz)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "specifically"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ea130024-b81a-4f9e-b2a3-e5b62a6e98c5",
                                                "sense_id": "f8af05f0-b5e0-325e-a8e8-fd90cb9a54d9",
                                                "text": "Aristotle […] calleth it [the soul] Entelechy, or perfection moving of it selfe (as cold an invention as any other) for he neither speaketh of the essence, nor of the beginning, nor of the soules nature; but onely noteth the effects of it […]."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "03262bb9-c229-44bf-99c4-d425a862cd80",
                                                "sense_id": "f8af05f0-b5e0-325e-a8e8-fd90cb9a54d9",
                                                "text": "Both Aristotle's concept of the entelechy and its modified role in Leibnizian \"monadology\" use the term in ways that could be applied in any being or \"substance,\" such as an amoeba or a tree, or even some one particular pebble viewed as being moved to fulfill the potentialities peculiar to its kind."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "454f0803-4af1-4677-81b0-68a0af2407a7",
                                                "sense_id": "f8af05f0-b5e0-325e-a8e8-fd90cb9a54d9",
                                                "text": "Students of Aristotle are familiar with his thesis that soul is the form of the body. In the De Anima Aristotle explains this further by claiming that the soul is the perfection or entelechia of the body (414a26)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "16aa233b-0527-419a-aadf-303ff5242a81",
                                                "sense_id": "f8af05f0-b5e0-325e-a8e8-fd90cb9a54d9",
                                                "text": "[T]his first acting principle, this entelechia is a real life principle (principium vitale) which has a perceiving ability as well, and which is imperishable. And this is just what I consider as the soul of animals. [Leibniz, 1710.] […] A body belonging to a monas which is its entelechia or soul, comprises together with the entelechia what we call a living thing, and together with the soul what we call animal. [Leibniz, 1714.]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "817b14f8-574b-3eb5-951a-fdabbe4d8880",
                                        "entry_id": "a79ebe71-42e2-3471-bc74-73737c1711c8",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A particular type of motivation, need for self-determination, and inner strength directing life and growth to become all one is capable of being; the need to actualize one's beliefs; having both a personal vision and the ability to actualize that vision from within."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "30e5631f-426d-4d5f-9ee6-6144026ea76f",
                                                "sense_id": "817b14f8-574b-3eb5-951a-fdabbe4d8880",
                                                "text": "The entelechy is the name given to our inner dynamic purpose. It is the seed of potential that nestles deep within us, containing the fractal image of who we really are and what we can become. The Greek philosopher Socrates first coined the term entelechy, and the great mystic [Pierre] Teilhard de Chardin brought it to public attention. […] The inner sense of purpose that is governed by the entelechy is the driving force behind our lives, helping us to blossom into the fullest expression of ourselves."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "89d54ada-e69b-41fb-be9f-ae507800c770",
                                                "sense_id": "817b14f8-574b-3eb5-951a-fdabbe4d8880",
                                                "text": "Karl Rahner and especially David Coffey, who follows and develops Rahner's theology, point out that the Holy Spirit bears an entelechy toward the Son. An entelechy (as the term is used by Rahner and Coffey) is an internal force or principle that drives a being toward its destiny. When applied to the Holy Spirit it refers to the redemptive purpose that motivates and orients the work of the Holy Spirit in redemption. The philosophical term entelechy helps to express the theology of the biblical description of the Holy Spirit as \"the Spirit of Christ.\""
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "d7570a61-e1f9-454f-8fbf-8458be0db08f",
                        "source_entry_id": "8ec3403d-4c73-3bf4-a59e-73328536cc3e",
                        "source_sense_id": "f05ed9b4-0749-340e-b764-d3c0050fc95d",
                        "source_sense_description": "Kraft, die in einem Organismus liegt und seine Entwicklung oder Selbstverwirklichung bewirkt oder lenkt",
                        "target_lang_word": "entéléchie",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "entéléchie",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "6eb85021-f1de-3299-8b64-63aec25ec33b",
                                        "entry_id": "c057fdee-f17f-36f8-9bdc-0123fea327cc",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Réalisation de ce qui était en puissance, par laquelle l’être trouve sa perfection (tradition aristotélicienne)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "695fb0d2-0a98-407e-a122-d702f2c8df04",
                                                "sense_id": "6eb85021-f1de-3299-8b64-63aec25ec33b",
                                                "text": "L’âme est l’entéléchie première d’un corps naturel doué d’organes et ayant la vie en puissance."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6183a224-30a6-456e-b50a-c83f85e79051",
                                                "sense_id": "6eb85021-f1de-3299-8b64-63aec25ec33b",
                                                "text": "On pourrait donner le nom d’Entéléchies à toutes les substances simples, ou Monades créées, car elles ont en elles une certaine perfection (échousito entelés), il y a une suffisance (autarkeia) qui les rend sources de leurs actions internes et pour ainsi dire des Automates incorporels."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "67804563-5676-3b99-b99d-7fdc52f95452",
                                        "entry_id": "c057fdee-f17f-36f8-9bdc-0123fea327cc",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Choses abstraites qui contentent l’esprit."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "literary",
                                            "pejorative"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7352deb1-7354-4840-aece-622be542f591",
                                                "sense_id": "67804563-5676-3b99-b99d-7fdc52f95452",
                                                "text": "Estimez vous heureux de trouver un amateur qui, de votre vivant, mettent en legs de cette X algébrique, de cette force galvanique ou de polarisation, de cette entéléchie, de cette sotte chose, quelle qu’elle soit, un prix très réel, le prix de votre ombre, auquel sont attachés la possession de votre maîtresse et l’accomplissement de tous vos vœux ;"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c61d3869-1575-4256-a119-e165b942967e",
                                                "sense_id": "67804563-5676-3b99-b99d-7fdc52f95452",
                                                "text": "Quant à Rousseau, il n’a fait qu’habiller des raisonnements et des systèmes. Julie et Claire sont des entéléchies, elles n’ont ni chair ni os."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "74a435ed-b5d9-40c9-b734-67c77533375c",
                                                "sense_id": "67804563-5676-3b99-b99d-7fdc52f95452",
                                                "text": "Dès mon retour d’Italie, je fus pris par le service militaire et envoyé à Nancy. Je n’y connaissais âme qui vive, mais je me réjouissais d’être seul, car il apparaissait ainsi plus clairement à mon orgueil d’amant et à Alissa que ses lettres étaient mon seul refuge, et son souvenir, comme eût dit Ronsard, « ma seule entéléchie »."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
